Subject: Let a compiled `new` teach the constructor its instance capacity

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_construct_done(ctor, inst)` feeds the constructed object's property count to the
    constructor's learned inline slot capacity (`js_learn_instance_cap`), exactly as the
    interpreter's construct paths do when a constructor returns.

Why we need it
  A constructor reached only through compiled `new` never learned its capacity, so every
  instance with more fields than the default grew its property array once per construction:
  the 12-field constructors paid a realloc and the engine's slow add for each of the last
  four fields. The interpreter had learned the capacity long before.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -62778,6 +62778,14 @@
     return 1;
 }
 
+/* A compiled `new` finished: feed the constructed object's property count back to the
+   constructor's learned instance capacity, as the interpreter's construct paths do, so the
+   next `this` is allocated with room for all its fields. */
+void qjs_aot_construct_done(JSObject *ctor, JSValue inst)
+{
+    js_learn_instance_cap(ctor->u.func.function_bytecode, inst);
+}
